WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.285
Transfer is the ability to apply learning beyond the original practice context.

00:00:04.285 --> 00:00:08.340
Near transfer happens when the new situation closely resembles what the person has already

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:08.882
practiced.

00:00:08.882 --> 00:00:12.820
Far transfer requires adapting principles to a meaningfully different situation.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.130
Expertise often transfers well when the underlying structure is similar,

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:17.410
the cues are recognizable,

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:20.720
and the expert understands which parts of the old model still apply.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.200
Expertise does not transfer perfectly everywhere.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:24.435
Domain boundaries matter.

00:00:24.435 --> 00:00:27.615
A strong performer in one technology, industry, organization,

00:00:27.615 --> 00:00:30.670
or operating environment may struggle when assumptions change.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.155
This is not a personal failure.

00:00:32.155 --> 00:00:35.680
It is a reminder that expert judgment depends on the conditions that shaped it.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.590
When those conditions change, the expert needs fresh evidence, updated models,

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:41.477
and sometimes deliberate re-training.

00:00:41.477 --> 00:00:45.435
Brittle expertise performs well under familiar conditions but struggles when novelty

00:00:45.435 --> 00:00:45.990
appears.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.870
It may depend on outdated assumptions, narrow examples,

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.880
or a lack of exposure to edge cases.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.000
Expert blind spots can also emerge because deeply skilled people may forget what beginners

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:55.600
do not know,

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.300
overlook weak signals outside their usual pattern,

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.180
or become overconfident when a situation feels familiar.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.920
Adaptable expertise requires humility and updating.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.370
Strong experts ask what has changed, which assumptions may no longer hold,

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.230
and what evidence would change their mind.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.795
They seek feedback from the environment and from other people.

00:01:11.795 --> 00:01:15.790
Continuous learning keeps expertise alive because real domains do not stand still.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:18.440
Tools, threats, markets, regulations, teams,

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.480
and technologies all change the conditions under which judgment must operate.
